course: dictionary
lesson: 4862
title: Ice: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The words around it tell you which meaning is right. «Ice or frozen peas should be wrapped in a cloth and used for about 15 to 20 minutes at a time.» The words wrapped, cloth and frozen peas tell you this ice is frozen water used to cool something, not a cake topping.
explain: «Much of Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets, huge sheets of ice covering land.» The words huge sheets and covering land are clues that ice means frozen water. To check, swap in the meaning: frozen water covering land still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Some words are used in phrases, and a phrase can mean something different from its single words. «Break the ice» does not mean smashing frozen water. It means helping people feel relaxed when they first meet. When a phrase seems strange, look at the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: «Ice» means water that has frozen hard because it is very cold.
fact: Water becomes ice at 0 degrees Celsius.
fact: To «ice» a cake means to cover it with icing.
fact: «Ice» is short for ice cream in some places.
fact: To «break the ice» means to help people feel relaxed when they first meet.
exercise: «Ice or frozen peas should be wrapped in a cloth and used for about 15 to 20 minutes at a time.» What does ice mean here? | Icing spread on a birthday cake | Water that has melted | *Frozen water used to cool something | Ice cream served in a cone
exercise: Ella's mum says, «Let's play a game to break the ice at the party.» What does break the ice mean? | To drop an ice cube on the floor | To smash a frozen pond | *To help people feel relaxed when they first meet
exercise: «She iced the cake with pink sugar.» What does iced mean here? | Ate it all at once | Cut it into cubes | *Covered it with icing | Put it in the freezer
test: «The advice is never to go onto ice on ponds, lakes, rivers or canals.» What is the advice warning you about? | Feeding the ducks | *Walking on ice on ponds and lakes | Swimming in summer
test: «An ice cube melts in your warm hand.» What happens to an ice cube in a warm hand? | It grows bigger | It catches fire | *It melts | It turns into a stone
exam: Sam says, «I am on thin ice with my teacher.» What does on thin ice mean? | Happy and calm | Standing on a very thin layer of frozen water | *In danger of getting into trouble | Very cold and shivering
